WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.644 --> 00:00:20.130
FAYE MOSS: Hello and welcome back to Superstars in History, the only talk show that features interviews with

2
00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:22.151
history's biggest stars.

3
00:00:23.011 --> 00:00:24.772
I'm your host, Faye Moss.

4
00:00:25.536 --> 00:00:33.900
Today's guest was an Olympic gold medalist and three-time World Heavyweight Champion who entertained his fans both in

5
00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:36.421
and out of the boxing ring.

6
00:00:37.242 --> 00:00:41.984
Please help me welcome one of the world's greatest athletes, Muhammad Ali!

7
00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:02.881
MUHAMMAD ALI: Faye, I think you've got that wrong.

8
00:01:03.582 --> 00:01:07.225
I'm not "one of the world's greatest athletes"—I am the greatest!

9
00:01:07.245 --> 00:01:10.628
FAYE MOSS: Well, I stand corrected!

10
00:01:10.648 --> 00:01:14.712
So can you tell us about your childhood?

11
00:01:15.633 --> 00:01:19.696
How did you become a boxer—I mean the greatest boxer?

12
00:01:19.736 --> 00:01:23.720
MUHAMMAD ALI: I was born in Louisville, Kentucky in 1942.

13
00:01:24.781 --> 00:01:28.544
My parents gave me a brand new, beautiful red bike for my twelfth birthday.

14
00:01:29.228 --> 00:01:32.749
I rode it downtown to see a fair, and when I came out of the building, my bike

15
00:01:32.769 --> 00:01:33.510
had been stolen.

16
00:01:35.351 --> 00:01:36.751
I was so mad I was crying!

17
00:01:37.571 --> 00:01:40.713
A policeman named Joe Martin saw me and asked what was wrong.

18
00:01:41.553 --> 00:01:44.434
I told him I wanted to beat up the person who stole my bike.

19
00:01:45.355 --> 00:01:48.616
He said if I wanted to beat somebody up, I needed to know how to fight.

20
00:01:49.416 --> 00:01:52.718
He was a boxing trainer and told me to come down to the gym he ran.

21
00:01:53.558 --> 00:01:54.458
FAYE MOSS: What a coincidence!

22
00:01:54.478 --> 00:01:56.679
MUHAMMAD ALI: It was.

23
00:01:57.479 --> 00:02:01.382
Joe was a white man, and at the time blacks and whites couldn't train together.

24
00:02:02.122 --> 00:02:03.503
But Joe's gym was integrated.

25
00:02:04.384 --> 00:02:07.606
Joe took me on, became my coach, and taught me how to fight.

26
00:02:08.466 --> 00:02:10.788
I won my first match a few weeks later.

27
00:02:10.808 --> 00:02:13.470
FAYE MOSS: You must have really loved it.

28
00:02:13.490 --> 00:02:17.713
MUHAMMAD ALI: No, I hated every minute of training, but I didn't quit.

29
00:02:18.693 --> 00:02:21.815
I knew if I suffered, I would live the rest of my life as a champion.

30
00:02:23.236 --> 00:02:26.018
Even then I knew I was great—and I wasn't afraid to say so.

31
00:02:26.859 --> 00:02:30.685
The newspapers loved that stuff, and it got me lots of attention.

32
00:02:30.705 --> 00:02:34.348
FAYE MOSS: And you also made up rhymes about your fights.

33
00:02:34.368 --> 00:02:37.090
MUHAMMAD ALI: I wrote poetry.

34
00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:42.514
My poetry got printed and quoted more than those poets the critics liked so much.

35
00:02:43.454 --> 00:02:50.179
I'll recite one for you: like a butterfly, sting like a bee.

36
00:02:50.779 --> 00:02:53.361
The hands can't hit what the eyes can't see."

37
00:02:54.262 --> 00:02:57.064
FAYE MOSS: That sort of sums up your boxing style, doesn't it?

38
00:02:57.836 --> 00:03:01.819
MUHAMMAD ALI: I used to dance around a lot and let my opponent get tired chasing me around.

39
00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:04.661
I trained hard so I could last longer.

40
00:03:05.562 --> 00:03:07.643
When the other guy was tired, I'd finish him off.

41
00:03:08.584 --> 00:03:11.907
At the 1960 Summer Olympics in Rome, I got a gold medal.

42
00:03:12.847 --> 00:03:19.412
Four years later, when I was 22, I won the title of World Heavyweight Champion from Sonny Liston,

43
00:03:19.692 --> 00:03:20.653
the big, ugly bear.

44
00:03:20.673 --> 00:03:24.276
FAYE MOSS: Around that time you changed your name.

45
00:03:25.292 --> 00:03:28.854
Before then you were known as Cassius Marcellus Clay, Jr.

46
00:03:29.754 --> 00:03:30.895
Why did you change your name?

47
00:03:30.915 --> 00:03:34.877
MUHAMMAD ALI: In the early 1960s I became interested in Islam.

48
00:03:35.838 --> 00:03:41.241
Many black Muslims were involved in civil rights causes and taking pride in our black heritage.

49
00:03:42.221 --> 00:03:47.864
Cassius Clay was a name that came from a white slaveholder, so I changed my name to show

50
00:03:47.904 --> 00:03:49.365
pride in my black heritage.

51
00:03:49.385 --> 00:03:54.948
FAYE MOSS: Things were going pretty well for you until you got drafted for the Vietnam War.

52
00:03:55.872 --> 00:03:59.975
When you refused to go into the army, you were arrested.

53
00:04:00.976 --> 00:04:01.777
Why did you do that?

54
00:04:01.817 --> 00:04:06.480
MUHAMMAD ALI: My religious faith prevented me from going to war and killing people.

55
00:04:07.321 --> 00:04:10.423
My conscience wouldn't let me go shoot some poor, hungry people.

56
00:04:11.284 --> 00:04:12.605
They never did anything to me.

57
00:04:13.345 --> 00:04:15.767
They were just little babies and women and children.

58
00:04:16.568 --> 00:04:17.969
How could I shoot those poor people?

59
00:04:18.869 --> 00:04:20.230
No, I couldn't do that.

60
00:04:20.671 --> 00:04:22.052
I said, "just take me to jail."

61
00:04:22.964 --> 00:04:26.987
I was sentenced to five years in jail, and that was okay with me because I believed in

62
00:04:27.027 --> 00:04:27.848
what I was doing.

63
00:04:28.868 --> 00:04:34.552
Finally my case made it to the Supreme Court, and the justices agreed that my faith prevented me

64
00:04:34.572 --> 00:04:37.695
from going to war, so they reversed my sentence.

65
00:04:37.715 --> 00:04:41.457
FAYE MOSS: But your heavyweight title had been taken away.

66
00:04:41.477 --> 00:04:46.621
And you weren't allowed to fight for three years while your court case was being heard!

67
00:04:47.462 --> 00:04:48.603
That must have been hard.

68
00:04:48.623 --> 00:04:50.444
MUHAMMAD ALI: No, not really.

69
00:04:51.276 --> 00:04:53.978
I was stopped from fighting, but I wasn't stopped from talking.

70
00:04:54.819 --> 00:04:58.002
I spent those years making speeches and visiting colleges.

71
00:04:58.923 --> 00:05:00.764
I spoke about my opposition to war.

72
00:05:01.725 --> 00:05:03.066
I spoke about Islam.

73
00:05:03.987 --> 00:05:04.988
I made a lot of friends.

74
00:05:06.029 --> 00:05:09.131
Eventually I was allowed to box again, and I got my title back.

75
00:05:10.132 --> 00:05:11.333
I was still the greatest.

76
00:05:12.874 --> 00:05:20.880
FAYE MOSS: You officially retired from boxing in 1981 with a record of 56 wins, 5 losses, and

77
00:05:21.361 --> 00:05:22.661
37 knockouts.

78
00:05:23.921 --> 00:05:28.022
You were not only great in the ring, you were also great out of it.

79
00:05:29.003 --> 00:05:33.244
In your later years you were well known for your humanitarian work.

80
00:05:34.144 --> 00:05:38.145
That's a long way from bashing people in the head in a boxing ring!

81
00:05:38.165 --> 00:05:43.347
MUHAMMAD ALI: Charity and good deeds were important to me as part of my faith.

82
00:05:43.367 --> 00:05:50.054
I always said that being the world heavyweight champion felt very small when I saw how millions of

83
00:05:50.134 --> 00:05:53.597
my poor black brothers and sisters were struggling in America.

84
00:05:54.597 --> 00:06:01.162
I visited historically black colleges and universities, and I donated money for scholarships for black students.

85
00:06:02.263 --> 00:06:08.048
Not only that, I traveled around the world to raise awareness on a variety of issues and see

86
00:06:08.108 --> 00:06:09.008
what good I could do.

87
00:06:10.129 --> 00:06:13.672
I went to places like Ghana, Iraq, and North Korea.

88
00:06:14.712 --> 00:06:18.175
One time I even stopped a man from jumping off a building in Los Angeles.

89
00:06:18.195 --> 00:06:24.579
FAYE MOSS: You also worked to raise awareness for Parkinson's disease, which affects the nervous system.

90
00:06:25.220 --> 00:06:28.542
More than ten million people around the world suffer from it.

91
00:06:29.362 --> 00:06:34.726
MUHAMMAD ALI: Yes, in the 1980s I was diagnosed with the disease, so it was a very personal

92
00:06:34.826 --> 00:06:38.929
issue. But I stayed healthy for a long time even after my diagnosis.

93
00:06:38.949 --> 00:06:42.652
FAYE MOSS: And you continued to do good work too.

94
00:06:43.596 --> 00:06:46.198
Thank you so much for talking with us today.

95
00:06:46.739 --> 00:06:48.761
We'll let you get back to history now.

96
00:06:48.781 --> 00:06:59.530
MUHAMMAD ALI: Thank you for having me, FAYE MOSS: In 1996 Ali lit the Olympic flame, and in

97
00:06:59.710 --> 00:07:03.734
2012 he was chosen as a bearer for the Olympic flag.

98
00:07:04.555 --> 00:07:10.180
During his lifetime Ali received awards and recognition for his humanitarian work.

99
00:07:10.996 --> 00:07:14.119
In 2016 he died of Parkinson's disease.

100
00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:22.027
World leaders and dignitaries from many countries praised him for his effort for peace and civil rights.

101
00:07:23.108 --> 00:07:29.154
Ali once said he wanted to be remembered "as a man who treated everyone right and helped as

102
00:07:29.354 --> 00:07:31.817
many of his people as he could."

103
00:07:32.818 --> 00:07:34.840
I think he accomplished that goal.

104
00:07:35.796 --> 00:07:40.539
I'll see you again next time with another one of history's greatest stars!
